{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>Their bars.</b> As the Targum renders: עַבְּרִין; and in la'az אשפ\"ריש." "<b>Five for the planks of the side of the Tabernacle.</b> These five are three — only that the upper bar and the lower bar are made of two pieces each: one runs to half the wall and one runs to half the wall; one enters the rings from this side and one enters the rings from that side, until they reach each other. So the upper and lower are two that are four; but the middle one — its length spans the entire wall, and it runs from one end of the wall to the other, as it says: ״וְהַבְּרִיחַ הַתִּיכֹן וְגוֹ׳ מַבְרִיחַ מִן הַקָּצֶה אֶל הַקָּצֶה״. The upper and lower ones had rings in the planks to enter into — two rings per plank, spaced within the ten cubits of the plank's height: one part from the upper ring upward, and one part from the lower ring downward, and each part is a quarter of the plank's length, with two parts between ring and ring, so that all the rings are aligned opposite each other. But the middle bar has no rings — rather, the planks are bored through their thickness, and it enters through the holes that are aligned one opposite the other; and this is what is said: ״בְּתוֹךְ״ the planks. The upper and lower bars on the north and on the south — the length of each is fifteen cubits; and the middle one, its length is thirty cubits — and this is ״from end to end״ — from east to west. And the five bars on the west: the length of the upper and lower ones is six cubits, and the middle one, its length is twelve, corresponding to the width of eight planks. So it is explained in the craft of the Tabernacle."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "the la'az is Old French esparmeins or similar, 'crossbars'"}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:54:52.763880513Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}